apples basic iPad has been updated for  2019 and while I'm still a little bit  disappointed with it for a couple of  reasons it's actually still the best  choice for people who need a basic but  very reliable tablet  the one thing Apple always upgrades with  each iPad update is the processor so I  was honestly shocked when I heard it's  packing the same 810 fusion chip from  last year's iPad but then I started to  think about why Apple would do this and  has started to make more sense to me  I'll explain why in a minute  now if you guys saw my video titled do  not buy the new 10.2 inch iPad I want to  explain the two reasons why I said that  first off the 810 chip is a lot slower  than the 812 and the new iPad Mini or  iPad air so I recommended users who want  a bit more power to spend the extra cash  on those iPads instead and the second  reason was that the nine point seven  inch iPad from 2018 was actually on sale  on Amazon brand-new for only 250 dollars  which is a much better deal for basic  iPad users since you get the same eight  n chip but now that sale is gone and  they're basically all sold out so with  that said the 10.2 inch iPad for three  hundred and twenty-nine dollars is now  currently the best deal for a reliable  budget tablet unless you're okay with a  much smaller screen if you pay just  fifty-five dollars more you can buy the  2019 iPad Mini five using the link in  the description which actually has a ton  of advantages over the budget iPad like  much better display quality the much  faster 812 chip and 64 gigabytes of  storage instead of only 32 on the basic  iPad the only reason I wouldn't buy the  iPad Mini is if either you don't want to  spend the extra cash for more  performance you don't want a small  display or if you want to use a  full-size keyboard with your iPad if you  haven't already seen our full comparison  of the 10.2 inch iPad 2 the iPad Mini 5  I recommend that you watch it by  clicking the button at the end of this  video now before I talk about why Apple  would keep the H on fusion chip for  another year let me first mention what's  new or what's not with the 2019 iPad the  biggest and most obvious change is the  large display but it didn't expect the  iPad itself to get so much bigger in  fact it's the same exact size as the  2019 iPad air and just like the air the  regular iPad now gets  smart connector so it supports the same  exact keyboard as air as well the only  problem is that it's $159 for that  keyboard and I can't imagine that most  people looking for a budget iPad will be  willing to spend that much more money  just for a keyboard it is currently on  sale for a hundred forty three dollars  on Amazon which is nice but it's still  really expensive a better choice for  people on a budget is this Bluetooth  keyboard case for only $39 I'll drop a  link to it down in the description below  other than that the only other  differences I've noticed were a bump up  to three gigabytes of RAM and improved  speaker quality besides that everything  else is basically the same the same 810  fusion chip as I've mentioned before the  same support for the first gen Apple  pencil the same 32 gigs of base storage  the same old Bluetooth 4.2 the same non  laminated display with a lack of  contrast gap between the display and  glass and high reflectivity so compared  to last year's iPad there really isn't  that much new this year but that's not  necessarily a bad thing now let me  explain why Apple would keep the 810  fusion chip around for another year the  budget $329 iPad was always meant to be  the budget tablet option for people who  need a basic tablet for regular tasks  like browsing the web using web-based  apps watching videos on YouTube  streaming shows and movies using the  Apple pencil for note-taking and  casually playing basic games on the App  Store and for all those things the a10  fusion chip is more than powerful enough  and Apple knows that basic iPad users  won't have any issues especially since  they added another gigabyte of RAM to  make sure the new multitasking in iPad  OS works smoothly but for the kind of  user who needs to do more serious work  on their iPad like advanced photo and  video editing or if they're planning to  play the highest end games available the  $329 iPad won't cut it and that's why  they have the higher-end iPads like the  iPad Mini and the iPad air which packed  the a12 chip and the iPad pro with the a  12 X and since there really isn't that  big of a difference in performance  between the a 11 chip and the a 12 there  would be a really huge boost in  performance if Apple packed the a 11  instead of the 810 in the budget iPad  there wouldn't be much of a reason to  spend more cash on the higher-end  iPads and that's what Apple chose to  stick with the a-10 for another year  until their other iPads are upgraded to  the a13 chip there's also a downside to  the three year old a10 ship that most  people don't think about starting with  the a11 Bionic chip Apple implemented  the knurl engine which powers machine  learning and augmented reality  applications that neural engine is  completely missing on the a10 so if and  when machine learning becomes more  common or if more augmented reality apps  come to the App Store those apps may run  significantly slower on this budget iPad  compared to the higher-end models so  that's something to think about I think  the biggest question most people are  having is how long this iPad will  continue to receive software support  seeing as the a-10 chip is already three  years old at this time in my opinion  since iPad OS is now its own separate  entity I think Apple will support the  2019 budget iPad for the next five years  as usual even though there's a chance  they might stop supporting the a10  packing iPhone 7 sooner than that  for example the iPhone 6 with the a8  chip doesn't support iOS 13 well the  iPad Mini 4 with that same a 8 chip does  support the new iPad OS so all in all  the 2019 10.2 inch iPad is still the  iPad to get if you're on a budget or if  you just need a basic iPad to do regular  things it's gonna be reliable for years  to come and that's what truly matters  when you're buying a tablet and if  you're struggling to choose between this  iPad and some of the higher-end models  check out that comparison video right  there to see all the differences and if  you enjoy this review click that circle  above to subscribe thanks for watching  and we'll see you in the next video  [Music]
 

